.TH CURL 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
curl \- make an HTTP request
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B curl
.RB [ \-X
.IR method ]
.RB [ \-H
.IR header ]...
.RB [ \-d
.IR data ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-iIfLs ]
.RB [ \-A
.IR agent ]
.I url
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B curl
makes an HTTP or HTTPS request through the computer's internet card and
writes what comes back to standard output. A URL without a scheme is
.BR http:// 's.
.PP
The card reaches only the public internet: never the server's own machine
or network (the server's settings may allow more, or less). It writes the
headers HTTP needs itself
.RB ( Host ,
.BR Content-Length ,
.BR Connection );
a request can't set those.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-X " method"
GET, HEAD, POST, PUT, DELETE, PATCH or OPTIONS.
.TP
.BI \-H " 'Name: value'"
A header; again for more.
.TP
.BI \-d " data"
A body: the request is a POST, with
.B Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded
unless a header says otherwise. Again for more, joined with
.BR & .
.TP
.BI \-o " file"
Writes to
.I file
instead.
.TP
.B \-i
Writes the response's status and headers first.
.TP
.B \-I
Makes a HEAD request: only its status and headers.
.TP
.B \-L
Follows redirects.
.TP
.B \-f
Fails on an HTTP error (400 or more): no output, exit 22.
.TP
.B \-s
Silent: no messages.
.TP
.BI \-A " agent"
The User-Agent header.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when done; 2 for bad usage; 6 for an unknown host; 7 when it couldn't
connect (refused, or the server forbids the address); 22 for an HTTP error
with
.BR \-f ;
23 when the output can't be written; 28 when it timed out; 47 for too many
redirects.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
curl https://example.com/
curl \-i \-X POST \-H 'Content-Type: application/json' \-d '{"a":1}' https://example.com/api
curl \-sL \-o page.html example.com
.fi
.SH NOTES
Real
.B curl
speaks many protocols and has hundreds of options; mcos's speaks HTTP and
HTTPS, with these.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wget (1)
